1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,730
Has this ever happened?

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:04,435
Shuffle a deck,
and look at the order.

3
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,765
The first card
can be any of 52,

4
00:00:07,945 --> 00:00:10,025
the next any
of the rest.

5
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,295
Multiply all
those choices,

6
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:13,905
and you count
the orders.

7
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,240
The answer
has 68 digits,

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,225
too big to say out loud.

9
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:20,865
Each shuffle is just

10
00:00:20,865 --> 00:00:23,175
one pick
from that huge pile.

11
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:27,545
Now give everyone alive
a deck, 8 billion of us.

12
00:00:27,565 --> 00:00:31,455
Shuffle once a second
for 14 billion years,

13
00:00:31,455 --> 00:00:33,060
the age of the universe.

14
00:00:33,785 --> 00:00:37,120
Even then, you cover
less than one part

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,525
in a trillion trillion
trillion of the orders.

16
00:00:40,805 --> 00:00:43,045
Your shuffle
is almost certainly

17
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:44,780
the first of its kind.

18
00:00:45,185 --> 00:00:47,720
Give the planet
1,000 times more

19
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,190
shufflers,
and it barely changes.

20
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,260
The pile of orders
is just too big.

21
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:55,920
Shuffle well,
and your order has

22
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:59,440
almost certainly
never existed before.

23
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:02,155
Not certain,
but a very safe bet.

24
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:03,920
Next envelope:

25
00:01:04,090 --> 00:01:06,885
all the water on Earth,
in one ball.
